# Line-ending policy.
#
# Batch files must be CRLF on disk: cmd.exe mis-parses some multi-line
# constructs (parenthesised `if` blocks) when a .cmd is LF-only.
#
# They are marked -text (i.e. stored verbatim) rather than `text eol=crlf`,
# because GitHub's "Download ZIP" serves the blob bytes and does NOT apply
# .gitattributes checkout conversion. With eol=crlf the blob stays LF, so a
# clone would get CRLF but the ZIP a user downloads would get LF — and the
# ZIP is the documented install route. Storing CRLF in the blob keeps both
# routes identical. tools/check_package.py asserts CRLF, so a stray LF
# save is caught before it can be committed.
*.cmd -text

# The runtime payload (schema + Lua) is copied verbatim into the Rime user
# directory, and the installer compares it by SHA-256. Pin it to LF in every
# checkout so a git clone and a release ZIP install byte-identical files.
*.lua text eol=lf
*.schema.yaml text eol=lf
